{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Submit an extension — extdir{% endblock %}
{% block meta_description %}Point extdir at an open-source Shopware 6 extension the crawlers have not found.{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
<div class="wrap page page--narrow">
    <div class="page-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Missing something?</p>
        <h1>Submit an extension</h1>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
        {# The honest version of "why isn't my plugin here". Each channel has a blind
           spot, and a maintainer who hits one should be told which, not left guessing
           whether they were rejected. #}
        <p class="lead">
            The directory finds extensions three ways: from Packagist, from GitHub topics
            like <code>shopware6</code>, and by searching repositories. An extension that
            is on none of Packagist, carries no topics, and falls outside those searches
            is invisible to all three. If you know of one, this is the way in.
        </p>

        <form method="post" action="{{ path('submission_submit') }}" class="report-form">
            <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('submit') }}">

            <p>
                <label class="label" for="url">Repository address</label>
                <input class="input" id="url" name="url" required
                       inputmode="url" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false"
                       placeholder="https://github.com/vendor/plugin">
                <span class="small subtle">
                    GitHub only for now — that is where the release and manifest data are read from.
                    Other forges are indexed, but not yet submittable.
                </span>
            </p>

            <p>
                <button class="btn btn--primary" type="submit">Check and add</button>
            </p>
        </form>

        <h2>What happens next</h2>
        <p>
            The repository's <code>composer.json</code> is read straight away. It is accepted if
            its type is <code>shopware-platform-plugin</code> and it has at least one tagged
            release — the same test every crawled extension passes, with no exceptions and
            nothing waiting on a human. If it qualifies you will land on its page within a few
            seconds.
        </p>
        <p>
            Compatibility, licence and maintenance signals fill in from there. Some arrive
            immediately, the rest at the next crawl.
        </p>

        <h2>This is not a claim of ownership</h2>
        <p>
            Anyone may point at a public repository; doing so asserts nothing about who holds
            the rights. If the extension is <em>yours</em> and you want to correct it, remove it,
            or have it marked as maintained by you, that is
            <a href="{{ path('my_extensions') }}">ownership verification</a>, which does need a
            sign-in and proof.
        </p>
        <p>
            Nothing is copied here. extdir indexes and links; the code, the releases and the
            images stay on your forge. See the
            <a href="{{ path('takedown') }}">takedown and correction policy</a> for how to have
            an entry removed.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
{% endblock %}
